research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-supersymmetry.md:696:## Source ledger and dated reading limits
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-supersymmetry.md:708:| Eric D'Hoker and D.H. Phong, *Lectures on Supersymmetric Yang-Mills Theory and Integrable Systems*, arXiv:hep-th/9912271v1 (29 Dec 1999), <https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9912271v1> | Full 125-page PDF retrieved; read §1.3, §§2.1–2.5, the N=1/N=2/N=4 multiplet decomposition and action construction in §§2.8–2.10, and §3.2 on holomorphicity/Wilsonian effective actions. | Source for N=2/N=4 field content, N=1 decomposition, prepotential/coupling conventions, and exact-SW context. The authors explicitly call AdS/CFT an equivalence conjecture; later exact/protected calculations do not prove a full continuum equivalence. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-supersymmetry.md:712:| N. Seiberg, “Naturalness Versus Supersymmetric Non-renormalization Theorems,” arXiv:hep-ph/9309335v1 (30 Sep 1993), <https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9309335v1>; “The Power of Holomorphy,” arXiv:hep-th/9408013v1, <https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9408013v1> | Full 14-page and 13-page PDFs retrieved; read Seiberg 1993 §§1–4 and 1994 §§1–2.2. | Supports the perturbative/nonperturbative and Wilsonian/1PI distinction. The 1993 argument explicitly assumes the theories exist as quantum field theories and that regularization preserves SUSY/global symmetries; it identifies nonperturbative superpotential corrections and IR holomorphic anomalies in 1PI. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-supersymmetry.md:713:| Steven Weinberg, “Non-Renormalization Theorems in Non-Renormalizable Theories,” arXiv:hep-th/9803099v1 (12 Mar 1998), <https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9803099v1> | Full 13-page PDF retrieved; read the action definition and complete spurion/symmetry/Feynman-graph proof through the gauge/FI qualifications. | Exact source for the candidate local perturbative Wilsonian F-term theorem: cutoff preserving SUSY and gauge invariance; holomorphic \(F(\Phi,W)\) unrenormalized except one-loop gauge-coupling renormalization; FI terms are separate. It does not cover nonperturbative corrections or arbitrary 1PI functionals. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-eft-experiments.md:7:## Existing material to reuse
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-eft-experiments.md:18:| `qftx-heavy-field-eft` and `qftx-heavy-field-eft-examples` | Published mathematical page with positive heavy-mode Gaussian data, a finite-dimensional heavy Gaussian shift, and a spectral low-momentum derivative-expansion remainder. The principal exact bound is `thm-qftx-heavy-derivative-bound`: for a positive self-adjoint heavy operator with source spectral support in `[0,E^2]`, truncating the inverse at order `N` has norm error at most `M^-2(E^2/M^2)^(N+1)`; its useful small-parameter regime is `E<M`. `prop-qftx-finite-gaussian-shell-semigroup` proves composition of normalized finite Gaussian shell integrations. These are precise mathematical models, not a derivation of generic interacting Wilsonian flow, generic SMEFT matching, or generic EFT truncation errors. **The published page additionally declares `requires: mixed-effects-and-longitudinal-models`; its relationship to this Gaussian-heavy-field content is not apparent from the current page/item statements. Assess that page-level edge explicitly; do not silently inherit it as an EFT prerequisite.** The cited item-level suppliers remain the relevant conditional claims. |
