        "statement": "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ and Minkowski signature $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-,+,+,+)$. Take QED with a real one-form photon potential $A=A_\\mu dx^\\mu$ on $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, field strength $F=dA$ with components $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, and two Dirac fields $\\psi_e,\\psi_\\mu$, each a four-component complex spinor-valued field, of charge $-e$ and positive masses $m_e<M_\\mu$. Use the minimal-coupling convention $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ieA_\\mu$ for charge $-e$, and the gauge kinetic term $-\\tfrac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}$. Define the dimensionless running coupling by $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)=e_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)^2/(4\\pi)$. The decoupling-subtraction (DS) convention uses minimal subtraction within each interval of active charged fields, removes a charged field from the low-energy theory below its threshold, and matches the coupling continuously at that threshold. Set $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(m_e)=\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}>0$ as the low-energy boundary condition. The source-reported one-loop DS flow has two active Dirac fields for $\\mu>M_\\mu$, one for $m_e<\\mu<M_\\mu$, and none for $\\mu<m_e$. If the one-loop solution remains in its positive-coupling domain, then for positive evaluation scale $Q$ of mass dimension one,\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(Q)}=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{Q^2}{m_e^2}\\right)\\qquad(m_e<Q<M_\\mu),$$\nand\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(Q)}=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{M_\\mu^2}{m_e^2}\\right)-\\frac{2}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{Q^2}{M_\\mu^2}\\right)\\qquad(Q>M_\\mu).$$\nThe two expressions agree continuously at $Q=M_\\mu$. These are solutions of the supplied one-loop renormalization-group equations; they are not measured values, exact all-orders predictions, or numerical error estimates.",
