## Statement

Let a local rigid four-dimensional N=1 superspace action datum have the form fixed above, including its canonical matter kinetic term, with finite-dimensional unitary matter representations of a fixed compact gauge group, an additional real D-term integrand $G$, a holomorphic gauge-invariant F-term integrand $F(\Phi,W)$, and a formal Wilsonian cutoff prescription at a nonzero scale $0<\lambda<\Lambda_0$. Assume the selected prescription has the local nondynamical-spurion extension specified in the action data and supplies the one-particle-irreducible cutoff vertex functional and a local derivative expansion coefficientwise in formal perturbation theory around the topologically trivial background, as specified above. Let $\langle\cdot\rangle_\lambda$ be the normalized coefficientwise expectation of the selected regulated formal expansion. Assume its rigid N=1 and BRST changes of variables obey $\langle\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}\mathcal O\rangle_\lambda=\langle\mathcal O\,\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}S_\lambda\rangle_\lambda$ and $\langle s\mathcal O\rangle_\lambda=0$ for every local insertion $\mathcal O$, with counterterms satisfying the same identities and no local gauge anomaly. Then the Wilsonian chiral integrand is unchanged coefficientwise, $F_\lambda(\Phi,W)=F(\Phi,W)$; the holomorphic gauge-coupling parameters may receive one-loop corrections, but no higher-loop corrections. If an unbroken U(1) factor is present, a Fayet–Iliopoulos parameter can receive a correction only at one loop, and that correction is proportional to the sum of the U(1) charges of all left-chiral multiplets, including multiplicities; it vanishes when this charge sum is zero. The theorem imposes no nonrenormalization condition on the additional real D-term integrand $G_\lambda$, the Kähler data, or coefficients expressed after a scale-dependent chiral-field rescaling that normalizes a positive real quadratic kinetic form. It is only a formal perturbative Wilsonian statement at nonzero cutoff: it does not assert nonperturbative exactness, a result for an arbitrary one-particle-irreducible functional, or existence of a continuum interacting quantum field theory.

## Statement references

[Weinberg, equations (1)–(12) and the Fayet–Iliopoulos argument](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9803099v1)

[Martin, §§4.7, 4.10, and 6.5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7)

[[def-qftx-wilsonian-n1-action-data|local N=1 Wilsonian action data]]

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A local rigid N=1 action datum, a formal high-mode split at $0<\lambda<\Lambda_0$, and the perturbative, locality, symmetry-preservation, and anomaly-free hypotheses in the statement.

[F1] [[def-qftx-wilsonian-n1-action-data]]: the bare density contains the canonical matter kinetic term $\sum_i\Phi_i^\dagger e^{-V_i}\Phi_i$, an additional real gauge-invariant $G$, and the chiral term $\tau\operatorname{Tr}(W^\alpha W_\alpha)/(8\pi i)+F(\Phi,W)$; the selected cutoff prescription explicitly assumes a local background-spurion extension, a local derivative expansion and one-particle-irreducible vertex coefficients, without proving generic partial integration has these properties.

[F2] [[def-qftx-nonabelian-brst-complex]]: the component gauge/ghost convention is $sA_\mu=\partial_\mu c+[A_\mu,c]$, $sc=-\tfrac12[c,c]$, and $s\Phi_i=-\rho_{i*}(c)\Phi_i$, extended as an odd derivation to finite jets.

[F3] [[lem-qftx-nonabelian-brst-nilpotence]]: for the stated finite-dimensional Lie algebra and representation data, the declared BRST differential satisfies $s^2=0$ on the finite-jet algebra.

[F4] [[def-qftx-n1-chiral-superfield]]: the supersymmetry generators are $Q_\alpha=\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha}-i\sum_{\mu,\dot\alpha}\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\alpha}\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}\partial_\mu$ and $\bar Q_{\dot\alpha}=-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}+i\sum_{\mu,\alpha}\theta^\alpha\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\alpha}\partial_\mu$; for external odd parameters, $\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}\Phi=\epsilon^\alpha Q_\alpha\Phi+\bar\epsilon^{\dot\alpha}\bar Q_{\dot\alpha}\Phi$.

[F5] [[def-qftx-wilsonian-n1-action-data]]: in a two-derivative scalar sector, $K(\phi,\bar\phi)$ is a real Kähler potential and $g_{i\bar j}=\partial^2K/(\partial\phi_i\partial\bar\phi_j)$ is its Hermitian metric; both are real D-term data, not holomorphic F-term data.

[A1] The perturbative expansion is formal and coefficientwise about the topologically trivial sector; no instanton or other nonperturbative contribution is included. The topological-angle density is treated as a total derivative under the stated boundary convention.

[A2] At every perturbative order the regulated local counterterms lie in the declared superspace action class and preserve the rigid supersymmetry and BRST/gauge Ward identities. Explicitly, for the normalized coefficientwise expectation, $\langle\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}\mathcal O\rangle_\lambda=\langle\mathcal O\,\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}S_\lambda\rangle_\lambda$ and $\langle s\mathcal O\rangle_\lambda=0$ for every local insertion $\mathcal O$. The selected quantum measure has no local gauge anomaly.

[A3] For the spurion selection argument, each independent coefficient of $F$ and $\tau$ is extended to a nondynamical chiral superfield, and each real coefficient of $G$ to an unconstrained nondynamical real superfield with positive scalar body on the local patch. The local counterterms are defined for these backgrounds before evaluating them at constant scalar components. Formal graph coefficients are analytic in these parameters there. The local background extension and its Ward identities are part of the selected prescription. A redundant parametrization of an external coefficient does not change any integrated field; no quantum Jacobian or holomorphic field rescaling is inferred.

